date-released: 2026-10-12
abstract: >-
  HELIOS-L1 is a desktop application for space weather. It reads live solar wind
  measurements from the spacecraft at the Sun-Earth L1 point (NOAA RTSW) and archived
  data from NASA CDAWeb (WIND, ACE, DSCOVR, SOHO, STEREO-A, Solar Orbiter, Parker Solar
  Probe), NOAA NCEI (DSCOVR plasma), OMNI, GFZ and NOAA SWPC, and runs published models
  on them: NOAA storm scales, magnetopause and bow shock positions, ring current (Dst)
  models, the auroral oval and NOAA's OVATION Prime aurora nowcast, CME arrival
  (drag-based model), shock normals and multi-spacecraft timing, minimum variance,
  de Hoffmann-Teller and flux rope fits, superposed epoch analysis, OMNI statistics
  since 1963, PFSS coronal fields and radio occultation geometry with SPICE. An event
  replay shows up to seven spacecraft on an Earth-fixed map with their measurements.
  Every result lists its data sources and can be exported. Builds for Windows, macOS
  and Linux.
repository-code: "https://github.com/jovian-explorer/HELIOS-L1"
keywords:
  - space weather
  - heliophysics
  - solar wind
  - geomagnetic storms
  - Sun-Earth L1
  - coronal mass ejections
